# Session-local working papers — not part of the deliverable
HANDOFF.md

# Local agent state and tooling. The skills this project was built with are credited by source and
# licence in docs/agentic-workflow.md §3; vendoring ~150 files of another project's repository into
# a ledger exercise is tooling, not deliverable.
.superpowers/

# `.claude/` is deliberately NOT ignored, so a decision to commit agent configuration stays a
# decision rather than something a blanket rule made silently. Two things currently sit there and
# are machine-local, not deliverable — check `git status` before staging:
#   .claude/worktrees/  — Claude Code's own worktree bookkeeping
#   .claude/skills/     — the vendored skill copies, removed from the repository on purpose
.claude/settings.local.json
.claude/worktrees/

# Build output
target/

# Reference material held on disk only — third-party documents, never committed.
docs/source/

# Tooling caches
.pytest_cache/
.serena/
__pycache__/

# Keys never land by accident. TestJwt now generates its signing key per-JVM instead of loading a
# committed PEM (see that class), so there is nothing left to un-ignore — recreating either filename
# would silently re-enable tracking, which is exactly what removing them was meant to end.
*.pem

# JMH run output — a measurement, not a source artifact
benchmarks/jmh-result.json
# maven-shade-plugin writes this beside benchmarks/pom.xml on every package
benchmarks/dependency-reduced-pom.xml

# Local credentials for DEVELOPER TOOLING (Sonar, and anything like it). This repository is PUBLIC:
# a committed token is a published token, and force-pushing does not unpublish what was already
# fetched or indexed — it has to be revoked instead. These patterns exist so that never happens.
#
# `.env*` is deliberately the broad form: it catches `.env`, `.env.sonar`, and anything else that
# starts that way (`.envrc`, `.environment`), so a new tool's credential file is ignored before
# anyone thinks to add a rule for it. That is the right default when the cost of missing one is a
# published secret.
#
# `!.env.example` MUST stay below it — git applies the last matching pattern, so the negation only
# works after the blanket. That file is committed on purpose and carries variable NAMES with empty
# values, never secrets; without this line the blanket would hide the one file whose whole job is to
# be read.
.env*
!.env.example

# Written by scripts/e2e/run-e2e.sh on every run, at the repository root (its EXIT trap
# resolves APP_LOG from here). Never a build artefact worth keeping.
app.log
# scripts/e2e/restart-replay.sh keeps the two halves apart on purpose: the log from before the
# kill -9 and the log from after the restart answer different questions when it fails.
app-before-kill.log
app-after-restart.log

# scripts/tls/gen-dev-ca.sh writes a THROWAWAY CA and leaf here for Traefik to terminate TLS
# with, locally and in CI. Private key material, in a PUBLIC repository: it is generated on
# demand and never committed, which is also why CI needs no certificate secret — it runs the
# same generator in-run. Ignoring the directory rather than the file extensions is deliberate:
# an extension list is a rule you have to remember to extend.
docker/tls/

# IDE
.idea/
